[?2004l[?2004h> - Rule 7: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j DROP
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ROOT CAUSE:
[?2004l[?2004h> The iptables rules attempt to filter HTTP/HTTPS traffic by matching the string "Mozilla" in packet payloads (likely targeting User-Agent headers). However, this approach fails because:
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. TCP connection establishment (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK handshake) occurs BEFORE any HTTP data is transmitted
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. The initial SYN packets from the client (10.0.0.5) contain NO payload data
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. The string match module cannot find "Mozilla" in empty packets
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. Traffic falls through to the DROP rules (rules 6-7), blocking the connection before it's established
[?2004l[?2004h> 5. The client hangs waiting for SYN-ACK that never arrives
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> WHY SSH WORKS BUT HTTP DOESN'T:
[?2004l[?2004h> SSH (port 22) has an unconditional ACCEPT rule with no string matching:
[?2004l[?2004h> - Rule 3: -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -j ACCEPT
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> This allows the TCP handshake to complete immediately, regardless of packet content.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> WHAT SHOULD BE CHANGED:
[?2004l[?2004h> The string matching approach is fundamentally flawed for connection-level filtering. String matching only works on established connections with payload data, but these rules prevent connections from being established in the first place. The rules should either:
[?2004l[?2004h> - Remove string matching and allow connections to establish first (rely on application-level filtering)
[?2004l[?2004h> - Use connection state tracking (which is already present for ESTABLISHED,RELATED) and allow NEW connections
[?2004l[?2004h> - Move filtering logic to the application layer (web server configuration) rather than iptables
[?2004l[?2004h> 
